<p class="sst-lead">Discretize the integral with a <strong>quadrature rule</strong> \(\{(w_i, s_i)\}\) &mdash;
deterministic nodes and weights, not random samples.</p>

<p>\[ \int_{\mathcal{I}} f(s)\, d\mu(s) \approx \sum_i w_i\, f(s_i). \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Stack the weighted, centered landmarks. The eigenproblem becomes a matrix factorization,</p>
  <p>\[ \sqrt{W}\,\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}^{\top}](\{s_i\}) = \widetilde{V}(\{s_i\})\,P(\tilde\sigma). \]</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p class="sst-claim">Because the curve is smooth, quadrature converges <em>spectrally</em> &mdash; far faster than
  the \(1/\sqrt{n}\) of Monte Carlo. You'll watch that convergence two scenes from now.</p>
</div>
